Statements:
[
    "Reaction-diffusion systems must maintain physical consistency through properties like mass conservation and quasipositivity to ensure well-posedness and physical realism.",
    "The paper presents systematic methods to embed these properties directly into parameterized reaction terms during the learning process.",
    "For a system with N species, a mass conservation condition can be represented as ∑ᵢ₌₁ᴺ cᵢfᵢ(u) = 0 for some constants cᵢ, ensuring that the total weighted mass remains constant.",
    "Given any class of parameterized functions F, a physically consistent version F̄ can be created by projecting each function onto the space of mass-conserving functions.",
    "The projection method modifies the original parameterized function fθ into a new function f̄θ where f̄θ(u) = fθ(u) - (∑ᵢcᵢfθᵢ(u)) * (c/∑ᵢcᵢ²).",
    "This ensures ∑ᵢcᵢf̄θᵢ(u) = 0 for all u, guaranteeing mass conservation.",
    "For each component n, if uₙ = 0, then fₙ(u) ≥ 0, ensuring that if a species concentration reaches zero, it cannot decrease further.",
    "A modification technique transforms general parameterized functions into quasipositive ones by introducing a positive corrective term that activates when a concentration approaches zero.",
    "For a given parameterized function fθ, a quasipositive version f̄θ can be constructed where f̄θₙ(u) = fθₙ(u) + max(0, -fθₙ(u)) * g(uₙ).",
    "g is a function that approaches 1 as uₙ approaches 0, ensuring non-negativity preservation.",
    "First apply quasipositivity modifications, then project the resulting functions to satisfy mass conservation.",
    "The modifications maintain sufficient regularity and growth conditions necessary for the well-posedness of the resulting PDE system.",
    "The approach extends existing theoretical results on regularization-based model learning to RD systems using these physically consistent reaction terms.",
    "Solutions to the modified learning problem (Pᵐ) converge to the unique solution of the original problem (P†) as shown through verification of necessary assumptions.",
    "This analysis-driven perspective distinguishes this approach from existing methods by addressing physical constraints directly at the level of reaction term parameterization, ensuring not only that the learned models align with physical principles but also that they produce well-posed RD systems with reliable and interpretable behavior."
] 

