Statements:
[
    "FLAME (Flow Enhanced Legendre Memory Models) achieves efficient and effective zero-shot forecasting for both deterministic and probabilistic time series tasks through the synergistic combination of Legendre Memory mechanisms and flow-based probabilistic modeling.",
    "This approach enables a lightweight yet powerful foundation model for general time series forecasting.",
    "FLAME utilizes two variants of Legendre Memory that provide complementary capabilities.",
    "LegT assigns uniform weights to the most recent history within a sliding window [t-θ, t].",
    "LegT is applied during encoding to compress varying-length local environmental information into fixed-size state vectors.",
    "LegT enables the model to capture intricate and varying local information (such as periodicities) that would be challenging to encode through traditional neural networks.",
    "LegS assigns uniform weights to all history [0, t], supporting compression of the entire history.",
    "LegS is used in the SSD-Decoder for making efficient long-term forecasts.",
    "The Legendre Memory approach provides mathematical guarantees for approximation accuracy, where larger numbers of Legendre polynomial bases lead to more precise approximations.",
    "FLAME employs a Normalization Flow-based forecasting head.",
    "The flow-based head models arbitrary intricate distributions over the forecasting horizon in a generative manner.",
    "The flow-based head uses MLP-based couple layers to achieve intricate distributional transformation while keeping the model lightweight.",
    "The architecture allows conditional parts to self-interact while preserving fine-grained causal relationships within each patch through an autoregressive approach.",
    "FLAME models are extremely lightweight (2M-10M parameters) compared to alternatives like Time-MOE (2.4B parameters) or Chronos (710M parameters).",
    "FLAME Large achieves the most 1st place rankings in both deterministic and probabilistic forecasting benchmarks.",
    "FLAME Small (2M parameters) achieves competitive performance with Sundial Base (128M parameters) while using only 1/64 of the parameters.",
    "Performance improves with increased sampling, reaching good results at around 100 samples without causing major efficiency burdens in real-world applications.",
    "The combination of the right inductive biases (Legendre Memory) with an efficient probabilistic modeling technique (Normalization Flow) can yield a time series foundation model that excels in zero-shot settings while maintaining exceptional computational efficiency."
] 

