    "Quantum canaries enable black-box auditing of quantum machine learning (QML) models through a novel approach that detects memorization and quantifies privacy leakage without requiring access to model internals.",
    "The method establishes a precise mathematical relationship between canary offset parameters and empirical privacy budget consumption.",
    "Quantum canaries are specially crafted quantum states designed to detect if a QML model has memorized specific training inputs.",
    "Offset-encoded quantum states are created using angle encoding schemes with controlled perturbations (offset parameter θ) where one encoding scheme ϕ₁ is applied to original data and a slightly different scheme ϕ₂ is applied to create neighboring states.",
    "Some quantum canaries are inserted into the training data and then reintroduced during testing to detect memorization behavior.",
    "The framework establishes a rigorous mathematical connection between the canary offset parameter and trace distance bounds, which directly relates to privacy budget consumption.",
    "The offset parameter α is mathematically linked to the trace distance upper bound d in the Quantum Differential Privacy (QDP) framework.",
    "For a Gaussian distribution of offset α ~ N(0, σ²), the standard deviation σ must satisfy: σ ≤ 2 arcsin(d)/2.576 to ensure a trace distance less than d with high probability.",
    "The algorithm computes an empirical privacy loss bound ε̂ₙ based on the statistical behavior of the model on canary vs. non-canary inputs.",
    "The Lifted QDP auditing algorithm operates without access to model internals.",
    "For each trial, the algorithm generates 2K canaries, with K canaries inserted into the training data.",
    "Two models are trained on slightly different versions of the data (θ₀ and θ₁), and their performance is compared on both types of canaries.",
    "Binary indicators of memorization (based on loss thresholds) are aggregated across trials using specially designed adaptive confidence interval estimators.",
    "The framework computes a lower bound ε̂ₙ on the privacy parameter ε with a guarantee that P(ε < ε̂ₙ) ≤ β, where β is a specified failure probability.",
    "This approach bridges the critical gap between theoretical privacy guarantees and practical privacy verification, making it possible to empirically validate QML privacy protections without accessing model internals."
] 

