    "Minimal input-perturbation-based counterfactual explanations (CFEs) are insufficient for clinical time series applications due to fundamental misalignments with the temporal nature and real-world constraints of healthcare interventions.",
    "The research highlights several critical limitations and proposes alternative approaches centered on temporal coherence and human-centered design.",
    "Most methods are designed with static data assumptions that don't account for the dynamic, temporally evolving nature of clinical time series.",
    "Simply perturbing inputs to change model predictions often creates implausible scenarios that violate underlying clinical and temporal constraints.",
    "There's a critical distinction between explaining model behavior and providing actionable recommendations for recourse - many methods focus only on the former.",
    "Current CFEs demonstrate poor robustness to even minor stochastic noise, making them unreliable in clinical settings where measurement variations are inevitable.",
    "Unlike tabular data, time series are inherently sequential - modifying one time step may disrupt temporal dependencies or produce unrealistic patterns, even if the perturbation is sparse.",
    "Standard pointwise proximity metrics are insufficient for time series data, as they don't account for global shape, alignment, and temporal dynamics.",
    "Current evaluation approaches for time series CFEs are fragmented, with too many metrics and too few accounting meaningfully for temporal structure.",
    "The research advocates for a shift toward counterfactuals that reflect sustained, goal-directed interventions aligned with clinical reasoning and patient-specific dynamics instead of arbitrary input perturbations.",
    "Ensuring that suggested changes unfold logically over time and maintain plausible temporal relationships.",
    "Different properties become essential depending on the system and user context, including sparsity, diversity, plausibility, and critically, actionability - whether users can interpret and implement the recommended changes.",
    "CFEs must be developed and evaluated within the context of their intended applications rather than using fixed evaluation sets.",
    "For clinical applications like digital health interventions supporting lifestyle changes in conditions such as hypertension management, CFEs must go beyond merely explaining predictions to providing genuinely actionable, purpose-driven interventions that are feasible in real-world healthcare contexts."
